	<text xml:lang="en">
		<body>
<div xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"><p>The global population appears to be decreasing, and the Bean Goose is currently listed as vulnerable on the Norwegian Red List <ref type="bibr" target="#b3">(Henriksen and Hilmo 2015)</ref>. As the Bean Goose is very shy and difficult to observe during the breeding season, DNA analyses of non-invasively collected molted feathers may be a valuable complement to traditional field-based approaches for counting and tracking Bean Geese. In this study we applied high-throughput sequencing to identify novel Bean Goose microsatellites. To facilitate genetic monitoring based on non-invasive sampling of molted feathers, we targeted microsatellite loci with short (\250 bp) amplicons, as amplification success and allele-dropout are affected by amplicon size when genotyping DNA from non-invasively collected samples <ref type="bibr" target="#b0">(Broquet et al. 2007</ref>). Furthermore, we developed two multiplex PCR assays for 12 loci and evaluated these assays in a Taiga Bean Goose (A. fabalis fabalis) population in central Norway. Finally, these two assays were applied to test for cross-species amplification success in four other Anser species.</p><p>Samples collected in central and northern Norway from seven Bean Geese were sent as blood stored in lysis buffer and DNA (extracted from feathers) eluted in tris-EDTA buffer to Ecogenics GmbH (Balgach, Switzerland) to produce an enriched microsatellite library and to test for polymorphism. In brief, size-selected fragments from genomic DNA were enriched for simple sequence repeat (SSR) content using magnetic streptavidin beads and biotin-labeled CT and GT repeat oligonucleotides. The SSRenriched library was analysed on a MiSeq platform (Illumina, Inc., San Diego, CA, USA) using the Nano 29250 v2 format. After assembly, 5307 contigs or singlets contained a microsatellite insert with a tetra-or a trinucleotide of at least six repeat units or a dinucleotide of at least ten repeat units. Suitable primer design was possible in 2305 microsatellite candidates, of which 36 were tested for polymorphism. These 36 loci were selected because they yielded amplicons of fewer than 250 base pairs, which facilitates analysis of non-invasively collected samples. Selection was then performed based on genetic variation, single-PCR performance, and peak interpretation, and multiplex PCR was developed through tests of different combinations of markers and adjustments of primer concentrations for the final subsets. Microsatellite loci were PCR-amplified with fluorescent-labeled forward primers. Single PCRs were performed in a final volume of 10 lL, with a protocol consisting of 95 °C for 15 min, 35 cycles of 95 °C for 30 s, 57 °C for 45 s, 72 °C for 45 s, and a final extension step of 72 °C for 30 min. Multiplexing was performed with a Multiplex PCR Plus Kit (QIAGEN, Hilden, Germany), following the manufacturer's protocol but using an 8.4-lL reaction volume. PCR products (0.8 lL) were mixed with GeneScan 500 LIZ (Applied Biosystems, Foster City, CA, USA) size standard (0.14 lL) and Hi-Di formamide (6.16 lL). Alleles were separated using capillary electrophoresis on an ABI 3130xl Genetic Analyzer and sizes assigned using GeneMapper software (Applied Biosystems).</p><p>For genotyping, we used sample types relevant for genetic monitoring and conservation genetics, i.e. blood from two individuals, plucked feathers from two individuals, and molted feathers from 27 individuals, all collected in the years 1990-2014 in central Norway. Cross-species amplification of the markers was tested by including samples (blood or tissue) from four other Anser species. Genomic DNA was extracted from blood using a manual commercial kit (QIAGEN DNeasy Blood &amp; Tissue Kit), following the manufacturer's protocol. DNA from feathers and tissue was extracted using an automated system (Maxwell Ò 16 Research Instrument; Promega Corp., Madison, WI, USA) and the Maxwell 16 tissue DNA purification kit, following the manufacturer's protocol.</p><p>The mean number of alleles, observed and expected heterozygosities, deviation from Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, and linkage disequilibrium were estimated using Arlequin version 3.5.1.2 <ref type="bibr" target="#b2">(Excoffier and Lischer 2010)</ref>. A Bonferroni correction for multiple statistical tests <ref type="bibr" target="#b8">(Rice 1989</ref>) was applied to linkage disequilibrium p values. Nullallele frequencies were estimated with Micro-Checker ver. 2.2.3 <ref type="bibr" target="#b13">(van Oosterhout et al. 2004)</ref>, and the probability of identity was calculated using GenAlEx 6.501 <ref type="bibr" target="#b6">(Peakall and Smouse 2012)</ref>.</p><p>PCR analysis of three to seven individuals was conducted for the 36 microsatellite loci, and allelic variation was detected in 34 of those (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). Scoring both PCR performance and allele interpretation (results not shown), we selected 12 loci for multiplex PCR development. The two final multiplex PCR assays were typed in 31 presumably unrelated Bean Geese [17 females and 14 males; sex determined with the Z-002A marker <ref type="bibr" target="#b1">(Dawson 2007)</ref>]. The  <ref type="table" target="#tab_2">2</ref>). Heterozygotes were present in both sexes for all loci, indicating that none are sex-linked. The mean expected heterozygosity was 0.75 (range 0.54-0.85) and mean observed heterozygosity was 0.74 (range 0.58-0.87; Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_0">1</ref>). After correcting for multiple tests, no pairs of loci displayed significant linkage disequilibrium. There was no indication of null-alleles in any locus, but one locus (Afa18) deviated significantly from Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium (Table <ref type="table" target="#tab_2">2</ref>). The combined probability of identity for the 12 loci was 6.5 9 10 -13 . Cross-species application of the two multiplex PCR assays revealed that all 12 loci amplified successfully in four other Anser species and all loci were polymorphic in the Greater White-fronted Goose (A. albifrons), Lesser White-fronted Goose (A. erythropus), and Pink-footed Goose (A. brachyrhynchus), while three loci were monomorphic in the Greylag Goose (A. anser; Table <ref type="table">3</ref>). The lower proportion of polymorphic loci in the Greylag Goose is in line with previous studies showing that microsatellite performance decreases with genetic distance (e.g. <ref type="bibr" target="#b7">Primmer et al. 1996)</ref>, as the Greylag Goose is most distantly related to the Bean Goose among the four Anser species tested <ref type="bibr" target="#b10">(Ruokonen et al. 2000)</ref>. In conclusion, these novel microsatellite loci and their multiplex PCR assays will be useful for family analysis, genetic monitoring, and population genetic analyses of the Bean Goose, as well as for other Anser species.</p></div><figure xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" type="table" xml:id="tab_0"><head>Table 1</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Characterization of 36 novel Bean Goose (Anser fabalis) microsatellite loci</figDesc><table><row><cell>Locus Primer sequences (5 0 -3 0 )</cell><cell>Primer</cell><cell>Repeat</cell><cell>n A Expected</cell><cell>Observed allele</cell><cell>GenBank</cell></row><row><cell></cell><cell>T m (°C)</cell><cell>motif</cell><cell>allele size (bp)</cell><cell>size range (bp)</cell><cell>no.</cell></row><row><cell>Afa01 F: TAAAGAGCAGAACGCAACAG</cell><cell>57.1</cell><cell cols="2">(TATC) 12 7 4 236</cell><cell>225-238</cell><cell>KT698169</cell></row><row><cell>R: TAATGGCAACTGTCCAAGGC</cell><cell>59.2</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>Afa02 F: CGAATCACGAGGCACAAGAG</cell><cell>59.7</cell><cell cols="2">(TATC) 11 5 6 209</cell><cell>196-216</cell><cell>KT698170</cell></row><row><cell>R: TCCATTTGTAGGTGAGAAAATATGC</cell><cell>58.4</cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell><cell></cell></row><row><cell>Afa03 F: A